# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# The Business of Belief: chapters 9-15.
from story1 import *

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ PART 4
flow(id="books",part=3,scope="world",
 title="The Books Nobody Has to Show",
 kick="American churches file no public accounts, a political group became a church on paper, and Italy was told to collect unpaid property tax.",
 sum="A treasury, a television network and a pilgrimage trade all produce money. In most countries an organisation that handles money on that scale has to show its accounts and pay tax. Religious bodies are often excused from both. The reasoning is old and has a serious side: a government that can tax and audit a faith can also use those powers to punish it. So the law in many places draws a line around religious bodies and leaves them alone.<br><br>The line has a cost. In the United States every other charity must file a public return each year that lists its income, its spending and what it pays its senior staff. Churches are not required to. A donor to a food bank can look up where the money went. A donor to a church cannot, unless the church chooses to say. The same exemption means that an organisation which can get itself classed as a church gains the right to close its books, whatever it actually does.<br><br>The second privilege is the tax itself. Land and buildings used for worship are commonly free of property tax. Religious bodies also own hotels, clinics, schools and rented flats, and the question of whether those should be taxed like anyone else's has been fought through the courts. This chapter shows the closed books in the United States, a tax case that reached Europe's highest court, and what the Vatican's own property office says it pays.",
 bridge="The first slide explains the American rule on church accounts and shows a political organisation using it. The second is a regional example from Italy, where the European Union's highest court ordered the government to recover property tax that church-owned businesses had not paid. The third returns to the Vatican's property office and the figures it now publishes on rent and tax.",
 out="An American church need not publish accounts, and a lobbying group gained the same right by being reclassified as one. In Italy a court ordered years of unpaid property tax recovered. Exemptions like these matter most to the bodies that own the most. The next chapter looks at the land.",
 rec="Religious bodies are often excused from publishing accounts and paying property tax, so the public cannot see, and does not share in, what they hold.",
 roles=["Where the books stay closed","Where the tax was ordered recovered","Where the ruling was made"],
 src=[{"t":"ProPublica, Right-Wing Think Tank Family Research Council Is Now a Church in Eyes of the IRS","u":"https://www.propublica.org/article/family-research-council-irs-church-status"},
      {"t":"ANSA, EU tells Italy to recoup unpaid property tax from Church","u":"https://www.ansa.it/english/news/2023/03/03/eu-tells-italy-to-recoup-unpaid-property-tax-from-church_29f3f2d7-6908-4b97-9e0e-5942de60cf82.html"}],
 steps=[
  st("2020-2022","Washington",WASH,2.8,"A Lobby Group Becomes a Church on Paper",
   "The last part followed money as it was raised. This part asks who is allowed to see it. In the United States a charity must file a yearly public return called Form 990. It shows income, spending, board members and the pay of top staff. Churches do not have to file it. The Family Research Council is an organisation in Washington that campaigns on abortion, marriage and other political questions. In 2020 it applied to the tax authority, the Internal Revenue Service, to be reclassified as an association of churches, and the request was granted. The news organisation ProPublica reported that once it had that status it no longer had to file the public return. Another campaign group, the American Family Association, made the same change in 2022. Forty members of Congress asked the tax authority to review the decision. The rule written to protect worship from the state now also shields political organisations from public view.",
   "https://www.propublica.org/article/family-research-council-irs-church-status",
   add={"United States":0},pins=[[WASH[0],WASH[1],"Family Research Council"]],pl=["frc","irs","afa","propublica"],ex=["frc2020"]),
  st("2018","Luxembourg",LUX,3.0,"Italy: Ordered to Collect the Unpaid Property Tax",
   "Regional Example: Closed books are one privilege. Untaxed property is another. Italy charged a local property tax called ICI. For years, buildings owned by religious and other non-commercial bodies were excused even when they were used to run businesses such as hotels, clinics and private schools. Competitors who did pay complained. The European Commission ruled in 2012 that the exemption was unlawful aid from the state, but said the money could not be recovered. On 6 November 2018 the European Union's highest court, the Court of Justice in Luxembourg, overturned that and told Italy to recover it. In March 2023 the Commission ordered Italy to collect the tax unpaid from 2006 to 2011. Press reports put the sum at four to five billion euros. A hotel owned by a religious order had been competing with the hotel next door while paying less tax, and every other taxpayer in the town made up the difference.",
   "https://www.ansa.it/english/news/2018/11/06/recover-ici-not-paid-by-church-says-ecj_eb419321-6492-4304-a041-248be2a1bc27.html",
   add={"Italy":1,"Luxembourg":2},arcs=[[LUX,ROME]],an=[["Luxembourg","Rome"]],pins=[[LUX[0],LUX[1],"EU Court of Justice"],[12.4964,41.9028,"Rome"]],
   pl=["ecj","cei","radicali"],ex=["ici2018"],reg=True),
  st("2023","Vatican City",ROME,3.0,"What the Vatican Says It Pays",
   "The Italian case was about church-owned businesses in general. The Holy See's own property office now reports its side. In its accounts for 2023, APSA said it paid Italy close to 6 million euros in property tax on buildings not used strictly for religious purposes, and a little over 3 million euros in company tax. Set that beside the portfolio from Chapter 2: 4,249 properties in Italy alone, of which 1,028, the largest ones, produce no rent. The office made a profit of 45.9 million euros that year. These figures are published because the Holy See chose, from 2021, to publish them. Before that, neither an Italian taxpayer nor a Catholic donor could have checked what was owned, what was let, or what tax was paid on it. The information that makes this slide possible has been public for five years, while the property has been held for centuries.",
   "https://www.americamagazine.org/faith/2024/07/29/vatican-investment-profit-248466",
   add={"Vatican City":0,"Italy":1},pins=[[ROME[0],ROME[1],"APSA"]],pl=["apsa"]),
 ])

flow(id="landlord",part=3,scope="world",
 title="The Landlord",
 kick="Religious endowments in India hold 940,000 acres, and the Church of England's fund is building thousands of homes on its land.",
 sum="Money given to a religion can be spent or saved. Land given to a religion usually stays. A field left to a church in a will, a shop pledged to a mosque, a village granted to a temple by a king: over centuries these gifts add up, because religious bodies do not die and rarely sell. In many countries the result is that religious institutions are among the largest landowners, holding farmland, city blocks and housing far beyond the ground their places of worship stand on.<br><br>The gifts were made for stated purposes, such as supporting worship, feeding the poor or paying for a school. The control, however, sits with a board, a trust or a set of commissioners. The people who worship on the land, farm it or rent homes on it are tenants and neighbours, not owners, and they seldom choose the managers. When land values rise, the managers decide whether to hold, lease or build.<br><br>This chapter gives two examples in which the figures are official. In India, Islamic endowments called waqf are overseen by government-appointed boards, and the government has published how much they hold. The figure is itself part of a political argument, and the slide gives both sides. In England, the Church Commissioners from Chapter 2 hold land as well as shares, and their latest report says how many houses are planned on it.",
 bridge="The first slide is a regional example from India, giving the government's count of waqf land and the objection that Hindu temple trusts hold more. The second is a regional example from England, where the church's investment body reports planning approval for thousands of new homes on its land.",
 out="Indian waqf boards hold 940,000 acres, Hindu trusts in three states hold more, and England's church fund is turning fields into housing estates. Land, shares, tax privileges and closed books make a strong institution. The next part asks what such an institution does when one of its own members is harmed inside it.",
 rec="Gifts of land made over centuries have turned religious bodies into major landlords, run by boards the worshippers and tenants do not choose.",
 roles=["Where the endowments hold land"],
 src=[{"t":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India, Waqf (Amendment) Bill 2024 explainer","u":"https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2024/sep/doc2024913394001.pdf"},
      {"t":"Deccan Herald, Waqf Bill and the bogey of land jihad","u":"https://deccanherald.com/opinion/waqf-bill-and-the-bogey-of-land-jihad-3201931"}],
 steps=[
  st("2024","New Delhi",DELHI,3.2,"India: 940,000 Acres Under Waqf Boards",
   "Regional Example: The last chapter showed property that goes untaxed. This one shows how much property there is. A waqf is land or a building given permanently by a Muslim for a religious or charitable purpose. Once given, it cannot be sold. In India, boards in each state oversee these endowments. In 2024 the Indian government said the boards controlled about 870,000 properties covering 940,000 acres, worth an estimated 1.2 lakh crore rupees. A lakh crore is a million million, so that is roughly 14 billion US dollars. The government called the boards the third-largest landowner in India, after the armed forces and the railways. Critics dispute that ranking. One pointed out that Hindu religious trusts in just three southern states hold more than 1,037,000 acres, which is more than all the waqf land in the country. Both figures make the same point about scale. Religious endowments of more than one faith hold land on the order of whole districts.",
   "https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2024/sep/doc2024913394001.pdf",
   add={"India":0},pins=[[DELHI[0],DELHI[1],"Ministry of Minority Affairs"]],pl=["moma","cwc","ttd"],ex=["waqf2024"],reg=True),
  st("2025","London",LONDON,3.2,"England: Thousands of Homes on Church Land",
   "Regional Example: India's endowments mostly hold what they were given. England's church fund develops what it holds. The Church Commissioners, who manage the £11.6 billion endowment from Chapter 2, own land as well as shares. Their report for 2025 lists, among the year's achievements, planning approval for more than 5,000 new homes to be built on church land. When farmland on the edge of a town receives permission for housing, its value rises many times over, and the gain goes to the owner. Here the owner is a religious body whose land came to it over centuries. The people who will buy or rent those homes, and the villages that will grow around them, deal with the Commissioners as they would with any property developer. The difference is that this developer's managers are appointed within the church, and its profits support an institution most of the new residents do not attend.",
   "https://www.churchtimes.co.uk/articles/2026/29-may/news/uk/uncertain-times-ahead-church-commissioners-annual-report-warns",
   add={"United Kingdom":0},pins=[[LONDON[0],LONDON[1],"Church Commissioners"]],pl=["commissioners","cofe"],reg=True),
 ])

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ PART 5
flow(id="institution",part=4,scope="world",
 title="The Institution Protected First",
 kick="Inquiries in France, Australia and the United States found the same thing: leaders shielded the institution and not the child.",
 sum="Every layer of the story so far has been about the institution's strength: its appointments, its treasury, its partners in government, its income and its property. This chapter is about the test of that strength. The test comes when a member, usually a child, is abused by someone the institution has placed in authority, and a parent or the victim reports it. At that moment the interests of the member and the interests of the institution point in opposite directions. The member needs the abuser removed and the truth told. The institution has a name, a body of clergy and a treasury to protect.<br><br>For decades the reports were handled in private. Over the past twenty-five years, courts and public inquiries in many countries have forced the files open. They were set up separately, in different legal systems, and looked at different churches. Their findings match. Accused clergy were moved to new posts. Victims were disbelieved or pressed to stay silent. Lawyers were consulted before the police. Lists of offenders were kept and not shared.<br><br>The numbers in this chapter are large, and each one is an official or independent count, not an estimate by a critic. They are included because they show how the structure described in the earlier chapters behaves under pressure. An organisation whose leaders are appointed from above and whose accounts are closed has no one inside it with both the knowledge and the power to act for the member. The slides give four inquiries, three into the Catholic Church and one into the largest Protestant body in the United States.",
 bridge="The first slide gives the American Catholic Church's own count of what abuse allegations have cost it. The second and third are regional examples from France and Australia, where independent commissions measured the scale. The fourth is a regional example from the Southern Baptist Convention, which shows that the pattern does not depend on having a pope or bishops.",
 out="Five billion dollars paid in the United States, an estimated 216,000 children abused by clergy in France, seven priests in every hundred accused in Australia, and a secret list of 703 names in Nashville. Four inquiries found leaders who protected the institution first. Children meet religious institutions in one more place, and the state often pays for it. That is the next chapter.",
 rec="When members were abused, inquiries on three continents found that religious leaders moved first to protect the institution's name, clergy and money.",
 roles=["Where the costs were counted","Where an inquiry measured the abuse"],
 src=[{"t":"National Catholic Reporter (RNS), More than $5 billion spent on Catholic sexual abuse allegations","u":"https://www.ncronline.org/news/more-5-billion-spent-catholic-sexual-abuse-allegations-new-report-finds"},
      {"t":"NPR, Top Southern Baptists stonewalled and denigrated sex abuse victims, report says","u":"https://www.npr.org/2022/05/22/1100616952/southern-baptists-sex-abuse"}],
 steps=[
  st("2004-2023","Washington",WASH,2.8,"United States: Five Billion Dollars",
   "The earlier chapters measured what religious bodies hold. This one begins with what one of them has paid out. A research centre at Georgetown University collects yearly surveys from the Catholic Church's American dioceses, which are the regions run by bishops, and from its men's religious orders. In January 2025 it added up twenty years. From 2004 to 2023 they reported spending 5.025 billion dollars on allegations that priests, deacons or brothers had sexually abused children. Three quarters went to victims. Seventeen per cent went to lawyers. Six per cent went on support for the accused. Over the same years they recorded 16,276 allegations they judged credible, most of them about abuse that began before 1980. The size of the bill gives a sense of how many cases had gone unanswered. Money that parishioners had given for the Church's work was spent settling claims, most of them about abuse that had happened decades earlier.",
   "https://www.ncronline.org/news/more-5-billion-spent-catholic-sexual-abuse-allegations-new-report-finds",
   add={"United States":0},pins=[[WASH[0],WASH[1],"CARA, Georgetown"]],pl=["cara","usccb","bishopacc"],ex=["cara2025"]),
  st("1950-2020","Paris",PARIS,3.2,"France: An Estimated 216,000 Children",
   "Regional Example: The American figure counts money. France counted people. In 2018 the French bishops set up an independent commission under a senior civil servant, Jean-Marc Sauvé. It worked for two and a half years. In October 2021 it estimated that 216,000 children had been sexually abused by Catholic clergy in France between 1950 and 2020. When abuse by lay staff in church schools and youth camps was added, the estimate rose to 330,000. The commission put the number of abusers among the clergy at between 2,900 and 3,200. Its chairman said that until the early 2000s the Church had shown a deep and even cruel indifference to the victims, and that the effort to shield clergy had been systemic, meaning built into how the institution worked. The commission was created by the bishops themselves. Its conclusion was that the institution they led had protected itself for half a century.",
   "https://www.irishtimes.com/news/world/europe/shame-and-horror-french-bishops-react-to-report-on-sexual-abuse-of-216-000-children-1.4692041",
   add={"France":1},pins=[[PARIS[0],PARIS[1],"CIASE"]],pl=["ciase","cef"],ex=["ciase2021"],reg=True),
  st("1950-2015","Sydney",SYDNEY,3.0,"Australia: Seven Priests in Every Hundred",
   "Regional Example: France estimated the victims. Australia counted the accused. A royal commission is the highest form of public inquiry in Australia, with the power to demand documents. One sat from 2013 to examine how institutions had responded to the sexual abuse of children. In February 2017 it reported figures gathered with the Catholic Church's cooperation. Of all the priests who had served in Australia between 1950 and 2010, seven per cent had been accused of abusing children. Between 1980 and 2015, 4,444 people reported abuse at more than 1,000 Catholic institutions, and the claims named 1,880 alleged abusers. The Church's own representative told the inquiry the numbers showed a massive failure to protect children. Seven in a hundred is not a few bad individuals. It is a rate that could continue for sixty years only if the people in charge kept choosing not to stop it.",
   "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/7-percent-of-australias-catholic-priests-accused-of-sexually-abusing-children",
   add={"Australia":1},pins=[[SYDNEY[0],SYDNEY[1],"Royal Commission"]],pl=["royalcomm","acbc"],ex=["royal2017"],reg=True),
  st("2000-2022","Nashville",NASHVILLE,3.0,"United States: A Secret List of 703 Names",
   "Regional Example: The first three slides concern a church run from the top by bishops. The Southern Baptist Convention is built the opposite way. It is the largest Protestant body in the United States, and each of its congregations governs itself. Its leaders long said that this was why they could not keep a register of abusive pastors. In May 2022 an outside firm hired by the Convention, Guidepost Solutions, published a 288-page report after 330 interviews. It found that for about twenty years a small group of senior leaders had stonewalled and belittled survivors who came forward, and had resisted reforms, in order to protect the Convention from being sued. During those same years a staff member had been keeping a private list of accused ministers. It held 703 names. Although the structure was different from Rome's, the decision was the same: the institution's legal safety was placed ahead of the members' safety.",
   "https://www.npr.org/2022/05/22/1100616952/southern-baptists-sex-abuse",
   add={"United States":0},pins=[[NASHVILLE[0],NASHVILLE[1],"Southern Baptist Convention"]],pl=["sbc","guidepost"],ex=["guidepost2022"],reg=True),
 ])

flow(id="schoolgate",part=4,scope="world",
 title="The School Gate",
 kick="In 75 countries the state funds religious schools, and in 64 religious instruction is compulsory in at least some public ones.",
 sum="A religion keeps its numbers in one main way: children are raised in it. A child does not choose a faith as an adult chooses a job. The belief arrives with the family, and in much of the world it also arrives with the school. That makes the classroom the most valuable ground a religious body can hold. A lesson taught to a seven-year-old by a trusted teacher, in a building the law requires the child to attend, reaches further than any sermon.<br><br>There are two routes into the classroom, and both run through the government. In the first, the state pays for schools that religious bodies own or control. The public funds the building and the salaries, and the religious body sets the ethos, often with the legal right to prefer its own members when admitting pupils or hiring teachers. In the second, the state's own schools teach religion as a required subject, with no secular alternative for families who do not share it.<br><br>This chapter joins this map to its companion on education, which follows who owns school and who writes what is taught. The site's own description of religion as a social mould fits here most exactly: instruction that begins before a child can weigh it, repeated for a dozen years, and paid for by taxpayers of every belief. Chapter 6 showed the same tool in its harshest form, in the boarding schools. The two maps in this chapter show its ordinary, everyday form, across most of the world.",
 bridge="Both slides are world maps drawn from the country reports of Humanists International. The first shows where the state funds religious schools. The second shows where religious instruction is compulsory in public schools.",
 out="Seventy-five governments pay for religious schools, and 64 require religious instruction in at least some of their own. A child taught this way grows into an adult member, and an adult member is also a voter. The next part shows what is done with those votes.",
 rec="Most members join a religion as children, and in much of the world the state pays for the schools, or runs the lessons, that bring them in.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t":"Humanists International, Freedom of Thought Report","u":"https://fot.humanists.international/countries/"}],
 steps=[
  st("Latest reports","Every country with a report",WORLD,1.15,"Where the State Funds Religious Schools",
   "The last chapter showed what can happen to a child inside a religious institution. This one shows how children come to be there. Humanists International records state funding of at least some religious schools in %(relschool_n)s of %(hi_n)s countries. In these places the public pays for the classroom and a religious body decides its character. In %(discr_n)s countries the report also finds that religious schools have the legal power to discriminate in whom they admit or employ. A family that lives beside a publicly funded school may find their child placed behind children of the right faith, and a qualified teacher may be refused a job paid for by the taxes that teacher contributes. For the religious body the arrangement is ideal. It receives a new generation of pupils every year, in a building it does not have to pay for, at the age when beliefs are formed.",
   "https://fot.humanists.international/countries/",data={"k":"relschool","only":None},pl=["humanists"]),
  st("Latest reports","Every country with a report",WORLD,1.15,"Where Religious Lessons Are Compulsory",
   "Funding a religious school is one route. The other is to teach religion in the state's own schools and make it compulsory. Humanists International records %(mand_all)s countries where religious or ideological instruction is mandatory in all or most state-funded schools, with no secular alternative. It records %(mand_some)s more where it is mandatory in at least some public schools. That is %(mand_n)s countries in all. In a further %(opt_n)s, state schools offer religious instruction with no secular alternative, though a family may withdraw the child. School attendance is required by law. In these countries, then, the law that puts a child in the classroom also puts the child in the religion lesson. The pupil is too young to judge what is being taught, and the parent who objects must single the child out to remove them. The membership counted in Chapter 1 begins here.",
   "https://fot.humanists.international/countries/",data={"k":"mandrel","only":None},pl=["humanists"]),
 ])

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ PART 6
flow(id="ballot",part=5,scope="world",
 title="The Pulpit, the Ballot and the Border",
 kick="American churches cleared to endorse candidates, a Japanese ruling party tied to one church, and $280 million sent abroad.",
 sum="A congregation is a group of people who meet every week, trust the person speaking and share a set of concerns. That is also a description of what every political campaign wants. A preacher who can deliver a room full of voters has something to trade, and a politician who can deliver laws that suit the preacher has something to offer back. The exchange does not need to be corrupt to be powerful. It only needs each side to understand what the other wants.<br><br>Most democracies have tried to keep some distance between the two. In the United States the rule since 1954 has been that a body which pays no tax, including a church, may not endorse or oppose candidates for office. The logic is that tax-free money should not be campaign money. In practice the rule was seldom enforced against churches, and in 2025 the tax authority itself told a court that it should not apply to what is said to a congregation during a service.<br><br>The trade also crosses borders. Religious campaign groups based in one country spend money to change the laws of others, on questions such as abortion and the rights of gay and transgender people. The groups are tax-exempt at home and do not name their donors. This chapter shows the American rule being set aside, a governing party in Japan found to have decades of ties with one church, and a count of religious campaign money leaving the United States.",
 bridge="The first slide explains the American rule against church endorsements and the 2025 court filing that narrowed it. The second is a regional example from Japan, where an assassination exposed the links between a church and the governing party. The third follows money from American religious campaign groups to four other regions of the world.",
 out="American churches may now endorse candidates from the pulpit, four Japanese ministers resigned over church ties, and 28 American groups spent at least $280 million abroad. That completes the chain from the headquarters to the ballot box. The final part of the story shows where people have broken it.",
 rec="A congregation is a ready-made bloc of voters, so parties and religious leaders trade support for laws, at home and across borders.",
 roles=["Where the rule was set aside","Where the party's church ties were exposed","Regions the money was sent to"],
 src=[{"t":"NBC News, IRS says churches can endorse political candidates without losing tax exemption","u":"https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/trump-administration/irs-says-churches-can-endorse-political-candidates-losing-tax-exemptio-rcna217483"},
      {"t":"openDemocracy, Revealed: $280m 'dark money' spent by US Christian Right groups globally","u":"https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/5050/trump-us-christian-spending-global-revealed/"}],
 steps=[
  st("2025","Washington",WASH,2.8,"Churches Cleared to Endorse Candidates",
   "The last part ended with children being brought into a faith. As adults they vote. In the United States a rule called the Johnson Amendment, written into the tax code in 1954, says that organisations which pay no tax, churches included, may not endorse or oppose candidates. A trade body of religious broadcasters and two Texas churches sued to overturn it. On 7 July 2025 the tax authority, the Internal Revenue Service, joined them in asking the court to settle the case. Its filing said the rule should not reach what a house of worship tells its congregation during a religious service. The law itself was not changed, and the rule had rarely been enforced against churches. But the body responsible for enforcing it had now said, in writing, that it would not. A donation to a church is still free of tax. The pulpit that donation pays for may now be used to tell the congregation how to vote.",
   "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/trump-administration/irs-says-churches-can-endorse-political-candidates-losing-tax-exemptio-rcna217483",
   add={"United States":0},pins=[[WASH[0],WASH[1],"Internal Revenue Service"]],pl=["irs","nrb"],ex=["irs2025"]),
  st("2022-2025","Tokyo",TOKYO,3.0,"Japan: A Governing Party and One Church",
   "Regional Example: In the United States the tie between pulpit and party is argued over in public. In Japan it was found by accident. Chapter 7 described the Unification Church and the money it took from its members. When former prime minister Shinzo Abe was shot dead in July 2022, the investigation showed that the church and the Liberal Democratic Party, which has governed Japan for most of the time since 1955, had been linked for decades. The reporting that followed found close ties between the church and many of the party's members of parliament, and four ministers resigned. The church had been collecting very large donations from Japanese families during the same decades. An organisation with friends in the governing party is unlikely to be looked at closely by the government. It took a killing to start the investigation that a court later said the facts had justified for forty years.",
   "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/court-in-japan-orders-dissolution-of-unification-church",
   add={"Japan":1},pins=[[TOKYO[0],TOKYO[1],"Tokyo"]],pl=["ffwpu","ldp"],reg=True),
  st("2007-2020","Washington",WASH,1.4,"$280 Million Sent Abroad",
   "Religious politics does not stop at the border. In October 2020 the news organisation openDemocracy read thousands of pages of the public tax filings of 28 American Christian campaign groups. These are groups that file returns, unlike the churches in Chapter 9. It found they had spent at least 280 million dollars outside the United States since 2007, on campaigns against abortion and against the rights of gay and transgender people. Europe received the most, almost 90 million dollars, followed by Africa, Asia and Latin America. None of the 28 names its donors. One group alone, the American Center for Law and Justice, spent 18 million dollars abroad, four fifths of it in Europe. A voter in Europe or Africa deciding on a law about family life may therefore be hearing arguments paid for by people in another country, whose names appear nowhere, through organisations that pay no tax at home.",
   "https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/5050/trump-us-christian-spending-global-revealed/",
   add={"United States":0},
   arcs=[[WASH,[4.3517,50.8503]],[WASH,[20.0,2.0]],[WASH,[100.0,20.0]],[WASH,[-60.0,-15.0]]],
   an=[["Washington","Europe"],["Washington","Africa"],["Washington","Asia"],["Washington","Latin America"]],
   pins=[[4.3517,50.8503,"Europe: almost $90m"],[20.0,2.0,"Africa"],[100.0,20.0,"Asia"],[-60.0,-15.0,"Latin America"]],
   pl=["aclj","adf","bgea","fwi","opendem"],ex=["opendem2020"],
   note="The lines run from Washington to the four regions the investigation names. They end at a point inside each region, not at a particular city, because the source gives totals by region."),
 ])

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ PART 7
flow(id="pushback",part=6,scope="world",
 title="Where People Pushed Back",
 kick="Irish voters struck blasphemy from the constitution, half a million Germans left in one year, and a Tokyo court dissolved a church.",
 sum="The chain described in this map is old and strong, but it is not fixed. Each of its links depends on something that people can withdraw. A blasphemy law depends on a constitution, and a constitution can be amended by a vote. A church tax depends on people remaining on the register, and a person can ask to be taken off. A religious corporation's privileges depend on its legal status, and a court can remove that status if the law allows.<br><br>None of these routes is easy. Changing a constitution takes a national campaign. Leaving a church can mean a break with family, and in the countries from Chapter 5 it can mean prison. Bringing a case against a wealthy institution takes years and money, and the institution can appeal. The examples in this chapter did not come from the top of any organisation. They came from voters, from members who decided they had seen enough, and from former members who took their evidence to a court.<br><br>The inquiries in Chapter 11 belong to this list as well, since most of them exist because survivors refused to stay silent. So does the Vatican's statement of 2023 in Chapter 6, which followed decades of Indigenous demands. This chapter adds three more: a referendum, a mass departure and a court order. They are included to show that the layers above a congregation rest, in the end, on the consent of the people below.",
 bridge="The first slide is a regional example from Ireland, where a referendum removed blasphemy from the constitution. The second is a regional example from Germany, where the number of people formally leaving the Catholic Church set a record. The third returns to Japan, where an appeal court upheld the order dissolving the Unification Church.",
 out="Two voters in three chose to end Ireland's blasphemy clause. More than 900,000 Germans left the Catholic Church in two years. Japan's courts stripped one church of its legal status. Each was a decision made from below. The last chapter sets these against the whole chain.",
 rec="Voters, departing members and courts have each removed a link from the chain: a blasphemy clause, a tax base and a church's legal status.",
 roles=["Where the public voted","Where members left","Where the courts acted"],
 src=[{"t":"RTÉ, Ireland votes to remove blasphemy from Constitution","u":"https://www.rte.ie/news/2018/1027/1007130-blasphemy-referendum"},
      {"t":"Adnkronos (Jiji Press), Tokyo High Court Upholds Unification Church Dissolution Order","u":"https://english.adnkronos.com/2026/03/04/tokyo-high-court-upholds-unification-church-dissolution-order/"}],
 steps=[
  st("2018","Dublin",DUBLIN,3.2,"Ireland: Two Voters in Three End the Blasphemy Clause",
   "Regional Example: Chapter 5 mapped 95 countries with blasphemy laws. Ireland used to be one of them. Its constitution, written in 1937, required that blasphemy be a criminal offence. On 26 October 2018 the question of removing the word was put to a national vote. The result was 64.85 per cent in favour, with 951,650 votes for removal and 515,808 against. The country's main churches welcomed the result, as did the campaign group Atheist Ireland, which had worked for ten years to have the vote held. The clause had rarely been used. Its removal still mattered beyond Ireland, because governments with harsh blasphemy laws had pointed to it as proof that Western democracies had such laws too. A rule that protects a religion from criticism can look permanent, but in Ireland it was removed once the public was asked.",
   "https://www.rte.ie/news/2018/1027/1007130-blasphemy-referendum",
   add={"Ireland":0},pins=[[DUBLIN[0],DUBLIN[1],"Dublin"]],pl=["atheistie"],ex=["ireland2018"],reg=True),
  st("2022-2023","Bonn",BONN,3.2,"Germany: Half a Million Leave in One Year",
   "Regional Example: Ireland changed a law. Germans changed their membership. Chapter 4 showed that a German pays church tax until they go to a government office and formally leave. In 2022, 522,821 Catholics did so. It was the highest number ever recorded, and it came after years of reports on abuse by clergy. About 380,000 people left the Protestant church the same year. In 2023 roughly 400,000 more Catholics left. Each departure is also a tax decision, because the person stops paying. The Catholic Church's income from the tax fell from its record of 6.85 billion euros in 2022 to 6.51 billion in 2023. No court or parliament was involved. The system gives a member exactly one lever, which is to leave, and in two years more than 900,000 Catholics pulled it. An institution funded through the tax office turned out to depend on its members after all.",
   "https://www.ncregister.com/news/half-a-million-people-left-the-catholic-church-in-germany-last-year",
   add={"Germany":1},pins=[[BONN[0],BONN[1],"Bonn"]],pl=["dbk","ekd"],ex=["exits2022"],reg=True),
  st("2026","Tokyo",TOKYO,3.0,"Japan: The Dissolution Order Takes Effect",
   "Regional Example: In Japan the lever was a lawsuit. Chapter 7 ended with the Unification Church appealing against the order to dissolve its Japanese branch. On 4 March 2026 the Tokyo High Court upheld the order, and it took effect that day. The church lost its status as a religious corporation, and with it the tax benefits that status carries. The lower court appointed a lawyer as liquidator, the person who takes control of an organisation's assets in order to pay what it owes. The church said it would appeal to the Supreme Court. This was the first time a Japanese court had dissolved a religious body for unlawful acts under civil law, as opposed to crimes. The evidence came from the people who had given the money. Former members, their families and their lawyers spent years assembling what the court then accepted.",
   "https://english.adnkronos.com/2026/03/04/tokyo-high-court-upholds-unification-church-dissolution-order/",
   add={"Japan":2},pins=[[TOKYO[0],TOKYO[1],"Tokyo High Court"]],pl=["ffwpu","tokyocourt","mext"],ex=["uc2026"],reg=True),
 ])

flow(id="whole",part=6,scope="world",
 title="The Whole Chain, and the One Thing It Cannot Own",
 kick="Every layer holds appointments, money, land or law. None of them holds a person's own experience of the sacred.",
 sum="The map has followed organised religion from its headquarters to the individual member. At the top, one office appoints the bishops of 1.4 billion people and one government controls entry to a holy city. Behind the altar is a treasury of buildings, gold and shares. Beside the headquarters stands the state: more than 80 governments name or favour a faith, 94 fund one, and 95 make it a crime to insult one. Where the state and a church worked most closely together, Indigenous children were taken into schools designed to end their own religions.<br><br>Further down the chain, preachers sell a promise of wealth and agencies sell the road to a shrine. Religious bodies hold land on the scale of districts, keep accounts the public cannot read and pay less tax than their neighbours. When members were harmed, inquiries on three continents found that leaders protected the institution first. The next generation is brought in through schools the taxpayer funds, and the adults it becomes are delivered to political parties as voters.<br><br>That is the business of belief named in the title: not faith itself, but the system of appointments, money, property and law built on top of it. Across the whole map there is one thing that none of these layers produces and none of them can own. It is what a person actually experiences when they pray, grieve, feel awe or sense that life has meaning. That happens in the person, on the person's own time. The final slides show how tightly governments still grip religion, how many people are stepping outside it, and how every layer of the chain can be found in a single country.",
 bridge="The first two slides are world maps from Pew Research Center: how far each government restricts religion, and how much hostility over religion comes from society itself. The third gives Pew's count of people who now belong to no religion. The final slide brings every layer of the map together in one country, the United States.",
 out="That is the chain, seen first from the headquarters and then followed down through the governments, sellers, owners and schools to the member. None of the relationships is hidden by law. They are hard to see together because they are spread across different countries and faiths, each with its own name. This map brings them into one place. What it leaves with the reader is the part no institution on it controls: a person's own sense of the sacred, which was there before any of these organisations and does not need their permission. From here, readers can open any organisation, country or documented record, or return to a chapter and follow the evidence again.",
 rec="Every layer of the chain holds appointments, money, land or law, and the one thing none of them owns is a person's own experience of the sacred.",
 roles=["The country used as the example"],
 src=[{"t":"Pew Research Center, Global Restrictions on Religion 2007-2022 dataset","u":"https://www.pewresearch.org/dataset/dataset-global-restrictions-on-religion-2007-2022/"},
      {"t":"Pew Research Center, How the Global Religious Landscape Changed From 2010 to 2020","u":"https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2025/06/09/how-the-global-religious-landscape-changed-from-2010-to-2020/"}],
 steps=[
  st("2022","Every country with figures",WORLD,1.15,"How Tightly Each Government Holds Religion",
   "The map has shown governments favouring, funding and policing religion. Pew Research Center combines all of that into one score for each country. Its Government Restrictions Index runs from 0 to 10 and counts laws and actions that limit religious belief or practice, including favouring one faith over others. This map shows the scores for 2022. Of %(gri_n)s countries and territories, %(gri_vh)s score 6.6 or above, and %(gri_h)s more score between 4.5 and 6.6. The highest are China at 9.1, Egypt at 8.4, Afghanistan and Iran at 8.2, and Syria and Indonesia at 7.9. The lowest are New Zealand at 0.3, the Marshall Islands and Micronesia at 0.4, and San Marino and Japan at 0.6. A high score can mean a government crushing all religion or a government enforcing one. Either way, the state has decided that belief is its business.",
   "https://www.pewresearch.org/dataset/dataset-global-restrictions-on-religion-2007-2022/",data={"k":"gri","only":None},pl=["pew"]),
  st("2022","Every country with figures",WORLD,1.15,"Where the Hostility Comes From Neighbours",
   "Governments are one source of pressure. The other is society. Pew's second score, the Social Hostilities Index, also runs from 0 to 10. It counts acts by private people and groups: mob violence, harassment over dress, attacks on converts, and conflict between religious communities. For 2022 the highest scores are India at 9.3, Nigeria at 8.7, Syria at 8.1, Pakistan at 7.9 and Iraq at 7.8. Compare this map with the one before. Some countries are high on both. Others differ sharply. China scores 9.1 for government restriction and 0.4 for social hostility. Nigeria is closer to the reverse, at 4.8 and 8.7. The two maps together show that a person can be held inside a religion, or punished for the wrong one, by the law or by the street. The blasphemy accusations in Chapter 5 work through both at once.",
   "https://www.pewresearch.org/dataset/dataset-global-restrictions-on-religion-2007-2022/",data={"k":"shi","only":None},pl=["pew"]),
  st("2010-2020","The whole world",WORLD,1.15,"One Person in Four Now Belongs to None",
   "Against all that pressure, people are leaving. Chapter 1 began with Pew's count that three people in four belong to a religion. The same study shows the fourth person. In 2020, 24.2 per cent of the world's population had no religious affiliation, which makes them the third-largest group after Christians and Muslims. Between 2010 and 2020 only Muslims grew faster. Christians grew in number to 2.3 billion but fell as a share of the world, to 28.8 per cent. Pew found that the main cause was not birth rates. It was people who had been raised as Christians ceasing to identify as Christian. People with no religion were a majority in ten countries and territories in 2020, up from seven in 2010. Pew also notes that in the United States more than half of the people with no religion still believe in God or a universal spirit. That suggests many have left the organisation while keeping some of the belief.",
   "https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2025/06/09/how-the-global-religious-landscape-changed-from-2010-to-2020/",
   pl=["pew"],w=1,ex=["pew2025"],note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the figures are world totals."),
  st("2026","Washington",WASH,2.6,"The United States: One Country, Every Layer",
   "Regional Example: The United States has no official religion, and it still contains every layer of this map. Its property law grew from a doctrine first set out in papal decrees. Its government paid churches to run 210 of the 417 boarding schools built to end Native religions. Its churches file no public accounts, and a political lobby gained the same right by being classed as one. A church investment fund hid a 32 billion dollar portfolio for twenty years and was fined 5 million. A Senate committee asked six television ministries where donations went and was not told. Catholic dioceses and orders paid 5 billion dollars over abuse claims, and Baptist leaders kept a secret list of 703 accused ministers. The tax authority now says the pulpit may endorse candidates, and 28 campaign groups sent 280 million dollars abroad. These are separate facts, each with its own source in the chapters above. Together they show the chain working in one place.",
   "https://www.doi.gov/pressreleases/secretary-haaland-announces-major-milestones-federal-indian-boarding-school",
   add={"United States":0},pins=[[WASH[0],WASH[1],"Washington"],[SLC[0],SLC[1],"Salt Lake City"],[NASHVILLE[0],NASHVILLE[1],"Nashville"]],
   pl=["doi","irs","sec","lds","senfin","usccb","sbc","frc"],reg=True,
   note="The three labelled points are places named in the earlier chapters this slide draws together: Washington, Salt Lake City and Nashville."),
 ])
